<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1434">1434</a>–<a href="/years/1737">1737</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Medici family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Medici was a Florentine banking family that dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1434, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo the Elder</a> returned from exile. Its members included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo the Magnificent</a> and the popes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Leo X</a> and Clement VII, and from 1532 they ruled as dukes of Florence and from 1569 as grand dukes of Tuscany. The main line died out in 1737, and the family's art collections remained in Florence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-medici.jpg" alt="House of Medici" width="320" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heralder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augmented_Arms_of_Medici.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Grand Duchy grew from the Florentine Republic, which the <b>Medici family</b> had dominated in the fifteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Venice, ruled by elected doges from the early 8th century, closed its Great Council in 1297 to the families then sitting in it, and Florence was governed by its guilds under the Ordinances of Justice of 1293 until the <b>Medici family</b> came to dominate it from 1434.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
